1
00:00:00,029 --> 00:00:04,460
In Core Inventory, this recorded
exercise returns the remaining issued

2
00:00:04,460 --> 00:00:06,500
stock and checks where the balance went.

3
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:10,220
Open On hand to review the
current item and location.

4
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:14,800
Use Items to select Academy training
batteries and choose Filter.

5
00:00:15,210 --> 00:00:19,610
The starting snapshot shows thirteen
at Station, five at Unit and one

6
00:00:19,610 --> 00:00:22,000
at Personnel: nineteen altogether.

7
00:00:22,399 --> 00:00:26,129
These figures follow earlier usage,
so they are later than the separate

8
00:00:26,129 --> 00:00:27,659
twenty-pack receipt example.

9
00:00:27,890 --> 00:00:29,190
Choose Issuances.

10
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:34,669
Find the training batteries row and
compare Location, Outstanding and Status.

11
00:00:35,050 --> 00:00:39,419
This row says Personnel, one
outstanding, and Partially returned.

12
00:00:39,879 --> 00:00:44,549
Personnel identifies the holder category
here; it does not print a person’s name.

13
00:00:45,030 --> 00:00:46,889
Choose the row’s Return action.

14
00:00:47,319 --> 00:00:51,510
The Return window names the batteries
and asks for Amount, Destination

15
00:00:51,510 --> 00:00:53,719
location, Status and Note.

16
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:56,569
Opening this form has
not recorded a return.

17
00:00:56,790 --> 00:01:01,529
For this example, Amount is one and
Destination location is Station.

18
00:01:02,089 --> 00:01:05,850
Read both before continuing: the
quantity is coming back from the

19
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:07,909
issuance holder to that destination.

20
00:01:08,490 --> 00:01:10,820
Add a useful Note explaining the return.

21
00:01:11,369 --> 00:01:15,760
Ours clearly marks a fictional software
exercise with no physical movement.

22
00:01:16,179 --> 00:01:18,910
Check the complete note rather
than relying on a shortened

23
00:01:18,910 --> 00:01:20,080
description elsewhere.

24
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:23,150
Status describes the condition
the equipment came back in.

25
00:01:23,689 --> 00:01:27,799
Out for repair is one available choice,
shown here without submitting it.

26
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:32,250
Damaged is another choice, also
left unsaved in this exercise.

27
00:01:32,689 --> 00:01:35,670
We restore In service for
the final training return.

28
00:01:36,069 --> 00:01:39,459
Select the condition that matches
your authorized record; choosing a

29
00:01:39,459 --> 00:01:43,379
software label does not establish
a physical inspection or repair.

30
00:01:43,590 --> 00:01:47,920
Review Amount, Station, In service
and the full Note together.

31
00:01:48,329 --> 00:01:51,250
When the intended return is
correct, select the Return

32
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:52,770
button at the bottom once.

33
00:01:53,249 --> 00:01:55,139
Read the resulting Issuances row.

34
00:01:55,509 --> 00:01:58,889
It now says Returned with
zero outstanding, and its

35
00:01:58,889 --> 00:02:00,179
Return action is gone.

36
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:05,360
That readback establishes the stored
result; the prepared form alone did not.

37
00:02:05,770 --> 00:02:08,419
Avoid submitting again
while waiting for a result.

38
00:02:08,619 --> 00:02:11,200
Choose History to check
the recorded movement.

39
00:02:11,579 --> 00:02:14,049
Filter Items to the
same training batteries.

40
00:02:14,489 --> 00:02:18,610
The new top Return row shows one
moving from Personnel to Station,

41
00:02:18,849 --> 00:02:20,339
with our complete training note.

42
00:02:20,740 --> 00:02:25,010
Earlier rows remain separate: Consume
records one used pack, and the

43
00:02:25,039 --> 00:02:29,230
older Issue row records two, with
Matthew Casey named in its note.

44
00:02:29,660 --> 00:02:34,320
Read the movement, quantity, direction
and note together so an older return

45
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:36,060
is not mistaken for the new one.

46
00:02:36,290 --> 00:02:38,499
Return to On hand for the balance check.

47
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:42,689
The fresh filtered table now
shows fourteen at Station, five

48
00:02:42,689 --> 00:02:44,830
at Unit and zero at Personnel.

49
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:46,790
The total is still nineteen.

50
00:02:47,340 --> 00:02:50,840
Returning the issued pack moves
its recorded holding; it does

51
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:54,610
not restore the pack consumed
earlier or create an extra receipt.

52
00:02:55,049 --> 00:02:58,619
Compare this result with the starting
balances and the new ledger entry.

53
00:02:58,839 --> 00:03:02,309
The example issuance is now
Returned with zero outstanding.

54
00:03:02,710 --> 00:03:05,030
Do not submit another return against it.

55
00:03:05,530 --> 00:03:10,100
For practice, use a different approved
issuance that still has stock outstanding

56
00:03:10,159 --> 00:03:11,850
and read its current balances.

57
00:03:12,289 --> 00:03:16,970
The same return and reconciliation
pattern serves fire and EMS, community

58
00:03:16,970 --> 00:03:21,539
paramedicine and mental health teams,
search and rescue, security departments

59
00:03:21,539 --> 00:03:26,379
and companies, wildland contractors,
and industrial emergency response teams.

